<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a metering conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A metering conveyor is a conveyor section that releases products into a downstream machine at a set rate and a consistent gap. Products arrive at irregular spacing, and the metering conveyor speeds up, slows down, or holds each product so the machine receives exactly what it can process. This prevents jams from over-feeding and starvation from under-feeding.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-1b">What is a metering belt?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1b" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A metering belt is the short, variable-speed belt at the end of a metering conveyor, directly before the machine infeed. A photoeye reads each product, and a VFD or servo drive changes the belt speed to set that product's gap and release timing. It is kept short so each speed change acts on one product at a time.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between metering and singulation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/singulation/">Singulation</a> separates touching or overlapping products into individual spaced units. Metering controls the rate and precise spacing of already-separated products. Singulation turns a mass into single file; metering controls how fast those single-file products feed into the next machine. Most packaging lines perform singulation first, then metering.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How does photoeye-based metering work?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A photoeye sensor detects each product on the metering belt. The control system measures the gap between consecutive products and adjusts belt speed to create the target gap before the product reaches the downstream machine. The photoeye provides real-time feedback that corrects spacing errors on every product cycle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What is servo-driven metering?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Servo-driven metering uses a servo motor to control the belt with high-precision speed and position control. The servo accelerates or decelerates for each product individually, holding tighter gap and position accuracy than VFD-based metering. Servo metering synchronizes with machine cycles for timing-critical applications like case packing and cartoning.</p>

</div>
</div>
